LU and PLU Factorizations Gaussian elimination solves one system \A\cvec{x} = \cvec{b}. In practice the matrix often stays fixed while the right-hand side changes: the same network under different loads, the same model fed different data. The row operations depend only on \A, so repeating them for every \cvec{b} wastes almost all of the work. This section stores the elimination as a factorization \A = \L\U, a lower triangular matrix that remembers the operations times an upper triangular matrix that is their result. We prove when it exists and when it is unique, and we repair its failures with row swaps. Throughout, F is a field and all matrices are square, in M_n(F). Triangular systems are cheap Some systems need no elimination at all. Take \U =  egin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 & -1 \ 0 & 3 & 1 \ 0 & 0 & -2 \end{pmatrix}, \qquad \U\cvec{x} =  egin{pmatrix} -1 \ -1 \ -4 \end{pmatrix}. The last equation mentions only x_3: -2x_3 = -4, so x_3 = 2. The second mentions x_2 and the known x_3: 3x_2 + 2 = -1, so x_2 = -1. The first then gives 2x_1 - 1 - 2 = -1, so x_1 = 1. This is back substitution: in an upper triangular system with nonzero diagonal entries, each equation introduces exactly one new unknown, read from the bottom up. A lower triangular system is solved the same way from the top down, by forward substitution. Now suppose a matrix \A factors as \A = \L\U with \L lower triangular and \U upper triangular. Then \A\cvec{x} = \cvec{b} reads \L(\U\cvec{x}) = \cvec{b}. Naming the inner vector \cvec{y} = \U\cvec{x} splits the problem in two: 	ext{first solve } \L\cvec{y} = \cvec{b} 	ext{ for } \cvec{y}, \qquad 	ext{then solve } \U\cvec{x} = \cvec{y} 	ext{ for } \cvec{x} . Both are triangular, so both are cheap. The expensive part, finding \L and \U, is done once for all right-hand sides. An LU factorization is Gaussian elimination written down once, so that it never has to be done again. LU factorizations [LU factorization] A matrix \L \in M_n(F) is unit lower triangular if it is lower triangular and every diagonal entry equals 1. Let \A \in M_n(F). An LU factorization of \A is a pair (\L, \U) of matrices in M_n(F) such that \A = \L\U, \qquad \L 	ext{ is 	extbf{unit} lower triangular}, \qquad \U 	ext{ is upper triangular}. In words: \L has 1’s on the diagonal and zeros above it, and the entries below the diagonal are unrestricted. \U has zeros strictly below the diagonal, and its diagonal entries are unrestricted; some of them may be 0. The definition says an factorization: whether there is only one is a theorem, and it needs a hypothesis. [An LU factorization from elimination] Let \A =  egin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 & -1 \ 4 & 5 & -1 \ -2 & 8 & 2 \end{pmatrix} \in M_3(
Q). Reduce \A to upper triangular form using only operations “subtract a multiple of a higher row from a lower row”, and record each multiplier. Compare the result with the matrix \U of the previous subsection. The first pivot is 2. To clear column 1 below it we subtract \ell_{21} = 4/2 = 2 times row 1 from row 2, and \ell_{31} = -2/2 = -1 times row 1 from row 3:  egin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 & -1 \ 4 & 5 & -1 \ -2 & 8 & 2 \end{pmatrix} \xrightarrow[R_3 	o R_3 - (-1)R_1]{R_2 	o R_2 - 2R_1}  egin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 & -1 \ 0 & 3 & 1 \ 0 & 9 & 1 \end{pmatrix}. The second pivot is 3. We subtract \ell_{32} = 9/3 = 3 times row 2 from row 3:  egin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 & -1 \ 0 & 3 & 1 \ 0 & 9 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \xrightarrow{R_3 	o R_3 - 3R_2}  egin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 & -1 \ 0 & 3 & 1 \ 0 & 0 & -2 \end{pmatrix} = \U. Put the multipliers below the diagonal of a unit lower triangular matrix, each in the position of the entry it cleared: \L =  egin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \ \ell_{21} & 1 & 0 \ \ell_{31} & \ell_{32} & 1 \end{pmatrix} =  egin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \ 2 & 1 & 0 \ -1 & 3 & 1 \end{pmatrix}. Multiplying out, row by row, \L\U =  egin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 & -1 \ 2 \cdot 2 & 2 \cdot 1 + 3 & 2 \cdot (-1) + 1 \ -2 & -1 + 9 & 1 + 3 - 2 \end{pmatrix} =  egin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 & -1 \ 4 & 5 & -1 \ -2 & 8 & 2 \end{pmatrix} = \A . So (\L, \U) is an LU factorization of \A, and \U is exactly the triangular matrix of the back substitution above. The example suggests the general statement: elimination produces \U, and the multipliers, with their signs as subtracted, fill in \L. We prove this below. First, the smallest cases. - Upper triangular \A. \A = \I \cdot \A, and \I is unit lower triangular. So every upper triangular matrix, including \O and \I, has the LU factorization (\I, \A). - 1 	imes 1. Every (a) \in M_1(F) is upper triangular, so (a) = (1)(a). Nothing can go wrong here, and that is exactly why the 2 	imes 2 failure below is instructive. Nonexample by minimal change. The matrix  egin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \ 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix} has the LU factorization  egin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \ 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix} =  egin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \ 1 & 1 \end{pmatrix}  egin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \ 0 & -1 \end{pmatrix}. Change the top-left entry from 1 to 0, and everything collapses. [An invertible matrix with no LU factorization] Show that \A =  egin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \ 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix} \in M_2(F) has no LU factorization, over any field F, although \A is invertible. \A is invertible, since \A\A = \I_2. Suppose, for a contradiction, that \A = \L\U with \L =  egin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \ \ell & 1 \end{pmatrix}, \qquad \U =  egin{pmatrix} u & v \ 0 & w \end{pmatrix}. Then \L\U =  egin{pmatrix} u & v \ \ell u & \ell v + w \end{pmatrix}. Comparing (1, 1)-entries with \A gives u = 0. Comparing (2, 1)-entries gives \ell u = 1. But \ell u = \ell \cdot 0 = 0 by @thm-field-basic-properties, and 0 
eq 1 in a field. This contradiction shows that no LU factorization exists. What failed is visible: the (1, 1)-entry of any product \L\U is u_{11} alone, so a zero there forces the whole first column of \L\U to vanish. In elimination terms, the first pivot is 0 while the entry below it is not, and the only cure is to swap rows, which “subtract a multiple of a higher row from a lower row” cannot do. We return to this with permutation matrices. Why “unit”. Without the 1’s on the diagonal of \L, uniqueness would fail even for invertible matrices. If \A = \L\U and \D is any invertible diagonal matrix, then also \A = (\L\D)(\D^{-1}\U), where \L\D is still lower triangular and \D^{-1}\U still upper triangular. When \L is invertible and \D 
eq \I, this is a different pair, and over any field with more than two elements such a \D exists. For instance  egin{pmatrix} 2 \end{pmatrix} =  egin{pmatrix} 1 \end{pmatrix} egin{pmatrix} 2 \end{pmatrix} =  egin{pmatrix} 2 \end{pmatrix} egin{pmatrix} 1 \end{pmatrix} =  egin{pmatrix} 4 \end{pmatrix} egin{pmatrix} 1/2 \end{pmatrix} over 
Q. Fixing the diagonal of \L removes this freedom and matches what elimination does: it never rescales a row. Invertible does not mean “has an LU factorization”. The matrix of @exm-no-lu-factorization is invertible and has none. Conversely, a singular matrix can have one: \O = \I \cdot \O. Existence of an LU factorization is a property of the order of the rows, not of invertibility. Unit lower triangular matrices Elimination builds \L out of elementary matrices, and the uniqueness proof below divides one factorization by another. Both need the same facts: products and inverses of unit lower triangular matrices stay unit lower triangular. We also need to know exactly when a triangular matrix is invertible. The key observation is a statement about where the nonzero entries of a product can sit. [Unit lower triangular matrices are closed] Let \L, \L' \in M_n(F) be unit lower triangular. Then: 1. \L\L' is unit lower triangular; 2. \L is invertible, and \L^{-1} is unit lower triangular. Write \L = \I + \N, where \N has nonzero entries only strictly below the diagonal. Multiplying two such matrices pushes the nonzero entries further below the diagonal, so \N^n = \O. Then the geometric series 1/(1 + t) = 1 - t + t^2 - \cdots stops after n terms and gives the inverse exactly. Say that \X \in M_n(F) is p-low if x_{ij} = 0 whenever i < j + p. So 0-low means lower triangular, and 1-low means zero on and above the diagonal. If \X is p-low and \Y is q-low, then \X\Y is (p + q)-low. A matrix that is n-low is the zero matrix. By @def-matrix-multiplication, (\X\Y)_{ij} = \sum_{l=1}^{n} x_{il} y_{lj}. A term can be nonzero only if i \ge l + p and l \ge j + q, which together give i \ge j + p + q. So if i < j + p + q, every term is 0 and (\X\Y)_{ij} = 0. For the second statement, every pair of indices satisfies i \le n < 1 + n \le j + n, so every entry of an n-low matrix is 0. Write \L = \I + \N and \L' = \I + \N'. Since \L and \L' are unit lower triangular, \N and \N' are 1-low. For (a), \L\L' = \I + \N + \N' + \N\N' by @thm-matrix-multiplication-properties. Here \N + \N' is 1-low, since a sum of entries that are both 0 is 0, and \N\N' is 2-low, hence 1-low, by the Claim. So \L\L' - \I is 1-low, which says that \L\L' is unit lower triangular. For (b), let \K = \I - \N + \N^2 - \dots + (-1)^{n-1} \N^{n-1}. Multiplying out, the sum telescopes: (\I + \N)\K = \I + (-1)^{n-1} \N^{n} = \I, where \N^n = \O because \N^n is n-low by the Claim applied n - 1 times. The same computation gives \K(\I + \N) = \I, since \N commutes with its own powers. Hence \L is invertible with \L^{-1} = \K. Finally \K - \I = \sum_{p=1}^{n-1} (-1)^p \N^p is a sum of 1-low matrices, by the Claim, so \K is unit lower triangular. This proves the lemma. Transposing everything, the same statements hold for unit upper triangular matrices (upper triangular with 1’s on the diagonal). The next lemma decides invertibility of a triangular matrix from its diagonal, without any computation beyond back substitution. [Invertibility of a triangular matrix] An upper triangular matrix \U \in M_n(F) is invertible if and only if every diagonal entry u_{11}, \dots, u_{nn} is nonzero. (\Leftarrow) Suppose every u_{kk} 
eq 0, and let \U\cvec{x} = \cvec{0}. We show x_k = 0 for k = n, n-1, \dots, 1 in turn. Suppose x_{k+1} = \dots = x_n = 0 (nothing is assumed when k = n). Row k of \U\cvec{x} = \cvec{0} reads u_{kk} x_k + \sum_{j > k} u_{kj} x_j = 0, because u_{kj} = 0 for j < k. The sum vanishes, so u_{kk} x_k = 0, and x_k = 0 since u_{kk} 
eq 0. Hence \cvec{x} = \cvec{0} is the only solution of \U\cvec{x} = \cvec{0}, and \U is invertible by @thm-invertible-tfae. (\Rightarrow) We prove the contrapositive. Suppose u_{kk} = 0 for some k. For each j \le k, the entries of column j in rows i \ge k are zero: if i > j this is upper triangularity, and the only remaining case i = j = k is u_{kk} = 0. So the first k columns of \U lie in W = \Span(\cvec{e}_1, \dots, \cvec{e}_{k-1}). The list (\cvec{e}_1, \dots, \cvec{e}_{k-1}) is independent and spans W, so \dim W = k - 1 (for k = 1, W = \{\cvec{0}\} has dimension 0). By @thm-size-bounds (a), these k columns are linearly dependent, so the columns of \U are dependent, and \U is not invertible by @thm-invertible-tfae. This proves the lemma. Elimination without row swaps To prove that elimination always produces an LU factorization, we first need to say exactly which elimination we mean. It is Gaussian elimination restricted to one kind of operation: subtracting a multiple of a row from a row below it. Elimination without row swaps. Input: \A \in M_n(F). Put \A^{(0)} = \A. For k = 1, 2, \dots, n - 1, write \A^{(k-1)} = (a_{ij}) and do the following. - If a_{kk} 
eq 0, set \ell_{ik} = a_{ik} / a_{kk} for i = k+1, \dots, n. - If a_{kk} = 0 and a_{ik} = 0 for every i > k, set \ell_{ik} = 0 for i = k+1, \dots, n. - If a_{kk} = 0 but a_{ik} 
eq 0 for some i > k, stop: a row swap is needed. If we did not stop, obtain \A^{(k)} from \A^{(k-1)} by replacing row i with (row i) - \ell_{ik} \cdot (row k), for each i = k+1, \dots, n. If the loop finishes without stopping, we say elimination without row swaps succeeds on \A. The numbers \ell_{ik} (i > k) are its multipliers, and \U = \A^{(n-1)} is its result. In both nonstopping cases the new (i, k)-entry is a_{ik} - \ell_{ik} a_{kk} = 0: in the first case by the choice of \ell_{ik}, in the second because both terms are already 0. The multipliers are well defined: row k is not changed during step k, so it does not matter in which order the rows i > k are updated. The proof will write each step as a matrix. By @def-elementary-matrix, the elementary matrix of the operation R_i 	o R_i + cR_k (i 
eq k) is \I + c\E_{ik}, where \E_{ik} is the matrix unit with 1 in entry (i, k). We will write the matrix unit as a product of a column and a row, \E_{ik} = \cvec{e}_i \cvec{e}_k	p , which holds because \cvec{e}_i \cvec{e}_k	p has (p, q)-entry (\cvec{e}_i)_p (\cvec{e}_k)_q, equal to 1 if p = i and q = k, and 0 otherwise. We will use one more rule constantly: for a vector \cvec{v} \in F^n, the 1 	imes 1 matrix \cvec{e}_k	p \cvec{v} is the k-th entry v_k, which we treat as a scalar. In particular \cvec{e}_k	p \cvec{e}_j = 0 for j 
eq k. For 1 \le k \le n and a vector \cvec{v} \in F^n whose first k entries are zero, put \G_k(\cvec{v}) = \I + \cvec{v}\, \cvec{e}_k	p . Its only nonidentity entries are v_i in position (i, k), for i > k: it is the identity with the column vector \cvec{v} written into column k below the diagonal. So \G_k(\cvec{v}) is unit lower triangular. [Multiplier matrices] Let 1 \le k \le n, and let \cvec{v}, \cvec{v}_1, \dots, \cvec{v}_m \in F^n be vectors such that the first k entries of \cvec{v} are zero and, for each j, the first j entries of \cvec{v}_j are zero (m \le n). Then: 1. \G_k(\cvec{v}) is the product, in any order, of the elementary matrices \I + v_i \E_{ik} for i = k+1, \dots, n; 2. \G_k(\cvec{v}) \G_k(-\cvec{v}) = \G_k(-\cvec{v}) \G_k(\cvec{v}) = \I; 3. \G_1(\cvec{v}_1) \G_2(\cvec{v}_2) \cdots \G_m(\cvec{v}_m) = \I + \cvec{v}_1 \cvec{e}_1	p + \cvec{v}_2 \cvec{e}_2	p + \dots + \cvec{v}_m \cvec{e}_m	p. For (a), let i, j > k. By @thm-matrix-multiplication-properties, (\I + a\, \cvec{e}_i \cvec{e}_k	p)(\I + b\, \cvec{e}_j \cvec{e}_k	p) = \I + a\, \cvec{e}_i \cvec{e}_k	p + b\, \cvec{e}_j \cvec{e}_k	p + ab\, \cvec{e}_i (\cvec{e}_k	p \cvec{e}_j) \cvec{e}_k	p , and the last term is zero because \cvec{e}_k	p \cvec{e}_j = 0 for j 
eq k. Repeating this, one factor at a time, the product of the \I + v_i\E_{ik} = \I + v_i \cvec{e}_i \cvec{e}_k	p in any order is \I + \sum_{i > k} v_i \cvec{e}_i \cvec{e}_k	p = \I + \cvec{v}\, \cvec{e}_k	p, since \cvec{v} = \sum_{i > k} v_i \cvec{e}_i. For (b), (\I + \cvec{v}\, \cvec{e}_k	p)(\I - \cvec{v}\, \cvec{e}_k	p) = \I - \cvec{v} (\cvec{e}_k	p \cvec{v}) \cvec{e}_k	p = \I, because \cvec{e}_k	p \cvec{v} = v_k = 0. Swapping the signs gives the other order. For (c), we use induction on m; the case m = 1 is the definition. Suppose the formula holds for m - 1. Then \Bigl(\I + \sum_{j < m} \cvec{v}_j \cvec{e}_j	p\Bigr)(\I + \cvec{v}_m \cvec{e}_m	p) = \I + \sum_{j < m} \cvec{v}_j \cvec{e}_j	p + \cvec{v}_m \cvec{e}_m	p + \sum_{j < m} \cvec{v}_j (\cvec{e}_j	p \cvec{v}_m) \cvec{e}_m	p . For j < m, \cvec{e}_j	p \cvec{v}_m is entry j of \cvec{v}_m, which is zero because the first m entries of \cvec{v}_m are zero. So the last sum vanishes, and the formula holds for m. This proves the lemma. Part (c) is the heart of the matter: when the factors come in the order \G_1, \G_2, \dots, they do not interfere, and each vector \cvec{v}_j lands untouched in column j. [LU from elimination] Let \A \in M_n(F), and suppose elimination without row swaps succeeds on \A, with multipliers \ell_{ik} and result \U. Let \L be the unit lower triangular matrix whose (i, k)-entry is \ell_{ik} for every i > k. Then \U is upper triangular and \A = \L\U . In particular \A has an LU factorization. Each step of elimination is left multiplication by a product of elementary matrices, which collapses to one matrix \G_k(- oldsymbol{\ell}_k). So \U = \G_{n-1}(- oldsymbol{\ell}_{n-1}) \cdots \G_1(- oldsymbol{\ell}_1) \A. To get \A back, undo the steps in reverse order. Their inverses \G_k( oldsymbol{\ell}_k) then appear in the order \G_1, \G_2, \dots, which is exactly the order in which, by @lem-elimination-matrices (c), the multipliers drop into place. Let \A^{(0)}, \dots, \A^{(n-1)} = \U be the matrices produced by the algorithm. For each k, let  oldsymbol{\ell}_k = \sum_{i > k} \ell_{ik} \cvec{e}_i \in F^n, a vector whose first k entries are zero. Step 1: \U is upper triangular. We show by induction on k that in \A^{(k)} every entry below the diagonal in columns 1, \dots, k is zero. For k = 0 there is nothing to prove. Suppose it holds for \A^{(k-1)}. Step k changes only rows i > k, replacing row i by row i minus a multiple of row k. In columns j < k, the entry of row k lies below the diagonal (k > j), so it is 0 by the induction hypothesis, and the entries of row i in these columns stay 0. In column k, the new entries below the diagonal are 0, as noted after the algorithm. This completes the induction, and for k = n - 1 it says that \U is upper triangular. Step 2: each step is a matrix. Step k performs the operations “add -\ell_{ik} times row k to row i” for i = k+1, \dots, n. By @thm-row-op-is-left-multiplication, applying them one after another multiplies \A^{(k-1)} on the left by the corresponding elementary matrices \I - \ell_{ik}\E_{ik}. By @lem-elimination-matrices (a), their product is \G_k(- oldsymbol{\ell}_k). Hence \A^{(k)} = \G_k(- oldsymbol{\ell}_k) \A^{(k-1)}, and therefore \U = \G_{n-1}(- oldsymbol{\ell}_{n-1}) \cdots \G_2(- oldsymbol{\ell}_2)\, \G_1(- oldsymbol{\ell}_1)\, \A . Step 3: undo the steps. Multiply both sides on the left by \G_{n-1}( oldsymbol{\ell}_{n-1}), then by \G_{n-2}( oldsymbol{\ell}_{n-2}), and so on down to \G_1( oldsymbol{\ell}_1). By @lem-elimination-matrices (b), each one cancels the factor next to it, and we get \G_1( oldsymbol{\ell}_1)\, \G_2( oldsymbol{\ell}_2) \cdots \G_{n-1}( oldsymbol{\ell}_{n-1})\, \U = \A . By @lem-elimination-matrices (c), the product on the left of \U is \I + \sum_{k=1}^{n-1}  oldsymbol{\ell}_k \cvec{e}_k	p. Its (i, k)-entry for i > k is \ell_{ik}; its diagonal entries are 1; its entries above the diagonal are 0. That is, it is \L. Hence \A = \L\U with \L unit lower triangular and \U upper triangular, as claimed. So the multipliers are not something to be remembered separately: they are the lower triangular factor. When a computer performs elimination it stores each \ell_{ik} in the slot (i, k) that it has just cleared, and at the end the one array holds \L below the diagonal and \U on and above it. The matrix that performs elimination, \L^{-1}, is not \L with its multipliers negated. In @exm-lu-by-elimination the operations multiply \A on the left by \G_2(- oldsymbol{\ell}_2)\, \G_1(- oldsymbol{\ell}_1) =  egin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \ 0 & 1 & 0 \ 0 & -3 & 1 \end{pmatrix}  egin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \ -2 & 1 & 0 \ 1 & 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} =  egin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \ -2 & 1 & 0 \ 7 & -3 & 1 \end{pmatrix}, and the entry 7 = 1 + (-3)(-2) mixes two multipliers. This matrix is \L^{-1}, by Step 3 of the proof. It is \L, whose factors come in the order \G_1, \G_2, that has the multipliers cleanly in place. Find the LU factorization of \A =  egin{pmatrix} 2 & 4 \ 6 & 5 \end{pmatrix} over 
Q by elimination, and check it by multiplying. The pivot is 2 and the multiplier is \ell_{21} = 6/2 = 3. Subtracting 3 times row 1 from row 2 gives \U =  egin{pmatrix} 2 & 4 \ 0 & 5 - 12 \end{pmatrix} =  egin{pmatrix} 2 & 4 \ 0 & -7 \end{pmatrix}, and \L =  egin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \ 3 & 1 \end{pmatrix}. Check: \L\U =  egin{pmatrix} 2 & 4 \ 6 & 12 - 7 \end{pmatrix} = \A. Solving with an LU factorization Once \A = \L\U is known, each system \A\cvec{x} = \cvec{b} costs two substitutions. [Two right-hand sides, one factorization] Let \A =  egin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 & -1 \ 4 & 5 & -1 \ -2 & 8 & 2 \end{pmatrix} with the factorization \A = \L\U of @exm-lu-by-elimination. Solve \A\cvec{x} = \cvec{b} for \cvec{b} =  egin{pmatrix} -1 \ -3 \ -6 \end{pmatrix} \quad	ext{and}\quad \cvec{b}' =  egin{pmatrix} 3 \ 7 \ -2 \end{pmatrix}. First right-hand side. Forward substitution in \L\cvec{y} = \cvec{b}:  egin{aligned} y_1 &= -1, \ 2y_1 + y_2 &= -3 \Rightarrow y_2 = -1, \ -y_1 + 3y_2 + y_3 &= -6 \Rightarrow y_3 = -6 - 1 + 3 = -4 . \end{aligned} Back substitution in \U\cvec{x} = \cvec{y}:  egin{aligned} -2x_3 &= -4 \Rightarrow x_3 = 2, \ 3x_2 + x_3 &= -1 \Rightarrow x_2 = -1, \ 2x_1 + x_2 - x_3 &= -1 \Rightarrow x_1 = 1 . \end{aligned} So \cvec{x} = (1, -1, 2). Check: \A\cvec{x} = (2 - 1 - 2, \; 4 - 5 - 2, \; -2 - 8 + 4) = (-1, -3, -6) = \cvec{b}. Second right-hand side. \L\cvec{y} = \cvec{b}': y_1 = 3, y_2 = 7 - 2 \cdot 3 = 1, y_3 = -2 + y_1 - 3y_2 = -2 + 3 - 3 = -2. Then \U\cvec{x} = \cvec{y}: -2x_3 = -2, so x_3 = 1; 3x_2 + 1 = 1, so x_2 = 0; 2x_1 + 0 - 1 = 3, so x_1 = 2. So \cvec{x}' = (2, 0, 1), and indeed \A\cvec{x}' = (4 + 0 - 1, \; 8 + 0 - 1, \; -4 + 0 + 2) = (3, 7, -2). Since the diagonal of \U is 2, 3, -2, all nonzero, \U is invertible by @lem-triangular-invertible, \L is invertible by @lem-unit-lower-triangular-closed, and so \A = \L\U is invertible. Each solution found is therefore the only one. How much is saved? Count the arithmetic operations (+, -, 	imes, \div) for an n 	imes n matrix. At step k of elimination there are n - k rows below the pivot. Each needs one division for its multiplier, then n - k multiplications and n - k subtractions in the columns to the right of column k. With j = n - k, the total is \sum_{j=1}^{n-1} j(2j + 1) =  rac{n(n-1)(4n+1)}{6} \approx  rac{2}{3} n^3 . Forward substitution with a unit lower triangular \L uses n(n-1) operations, and back substitution n^2, so a pair of triangular solves costs 2n^2 - n. For n = 1000 that is about 6.7 	imes 10^8 operations for the factorization, done once, against about 2 	imes 10^6 for each new right-hand side. Uniqueness The factorization of @exm-lu-by-elimination came from one particular procedure. Could another procedure give a different pair (\L, \U)? For invertible matrices, no. [Uniqueness of the LU factorization] Let \A \in M_n(F) be invertible. If \A = \L_1\U_1 and \A = \L_2\U_2 are LU factorizations, then \L_1 = \L_2 and \U_1 = \U_2. Take the difference in multiplicative form: from \L_1\U_1 = \L_2\U_2 we get (\L_2^{-1}\L_1)\U_1 = \U_2. The matrix \M = \L_2^{-1}\L_1 is unit lower triangular, and multiplying \U_1 by it must leave an upper triangular matrix. Clearing below the diagonal column by column, the nonzero pivots of \U_1 force \M to have nothing below its diagonal. Invertibility of \A is what provides those nonzero pivots. By @lem-unit-lower-triangular-closed (b), \L_1 and \L_2 are invertible. Hence \U_1 = \L_1^{-1}\A is a product of invertible matrices, so it is invertible by @thm-inverse-matrix-properties, and by @lem-triangular-invertible its diagonal entries u_{11}, \dots, u_{nn} are all nonzero. Let \M = \L_2^{-1}\L_1. By @lem-unit-lower-triangular-closed, \M = (m_{ij}) is unit lower triangular. Multiplying \L_1\U_1 = \L_2\U_2 on the left by \L_2^{-1} gives \M\U_1 = \U_2, so \M\U_1 is upper triangular. We show m_{ij} = 0 for all i > j, by induction on the column j. Fix j, and suppose m_{ik} = 0 whenever k < j and i > k. Let i > j. Since \M\U_1 is upper triangular and i > j, 0 = (\M\U_1)_{ij} = \sum_{k=1}^{n} m_{ik} u_{kj} = \sum_{k \le j} m_{ik} u_{kj} = m_{ij} u_{jj}, where the third equality uses u_{kj} = 0 for k > j, and the last uses m_{ik} = 0 for k < j (here i > j > k). Since u_{jj} 
eq 0, we get m_{ij} = 0. This completes the induction, so \M is lower triangular with 1’s on the diagonal and nothing below it: \M = \I. Hence \L_2^{-1}\L_1 = \I, and multiplying by \L_2 gives \L_1 = \L_2. Then \U_1 = \L_1^{-1}\A = \L_2^{-1}\A = \U_2. This proves the theorem. Two consequences are worth saying. The LU factorization of an invertible matrix does not depend on how it was found, so it is legitimate to speak of the factors \L and \U. And if elimination without row swaps succeeds on an invertible \A, then every pivot is nonzero: row k is not changed after step k, so the pivot of step k is u_{kk}, which is nonzero by the first paragraph of the proof. So the second case of the algorithm (a zero pivot over a zero column) never occurs. The hypothesis “invertible” cannot be dropped. For every c \in F,  egin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} =  egin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \ c & 1 \end{pmatrix}  egin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}, since the first column of the right factor is zero. Taking c = 0 and c = 1 gives two different LU factorizations. The proof breaks exactly at “u_{jj} 
