R, computers do not choose just any nonzero pivot. They choose the entry of largest absolute value in the column, which is called partial pivoting. The reason is rounding. For \A =  egin{pmatrix} \varepsilon & 1 \ 1 & 1 \end{pmatrix} with \varepsilon = 10^{-20}, elimination without swaps gives the multiplier 10^{20} and u_{22} = 1 - 10^{20}, which in standard double-precision arithmetic is stored as -10^{20}. Multiplying back, the computed \L\U has (2, 2)-entry 10^{20} - 10^{20} = 0 instead of 1. Swapping the rows first gives the harmless multiplier \varepsilon. Chapter 24 makes this precise. Symmetric matrices: the LDLᵀ factorization When \A is symmetric, the two factors of \A = \L\U are secretly related: up to a diagonal matrix, \U is the transpose of \L. In @exm-lu-by-elimination the factors look unrelated, but that matrix is not symmetric. Try a symmetric one:  egin{pmatrix} 1 & 3 & -2 \ 3 & 7 & -8 \ -2 & -8 & 5 \end{pmatrix} \xrightarrow[R_3 	o R_3 + 2R_1]{R_2 	o R_2 - 3R_1}  egin{pmatrix} 1 & 3 & -2 \ 0 & -2 & -2 \ 0 & -2 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \xrightarrow{R_3 	o R_3 - R_2}  egin{pmatrix} 1 & 3 & -2 \ 0 & -2 & -2 \ 0 & 0 & 3 \end{pmatrix} = \U , with multipliers \ell_{21} = 3, \ell_{31} = -2, \ell_{32} = 1. Divide each row of \U by its diagonal entry: the rows become (1, 3, -2), (0, 1, 1), (0, 0, 1). These are the columns of \L =  egin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \ 3 & 1 & 0 \ -2 & 1 & 1 \end{pmatrix}, read as rows. So \U = \D\L	p with \D = \diag(1, -2, 3), and \A = \L\D\L	p. [LDLᵀ factorization] Let \A \in M_n(F) be symmetric and invertible, and suppose \A = \L\U is an LU factorization. Then the diagonal entries u_{11}, \dots, u_{nn} of \U are nonzero, and with \D = \diag(u_{11}, \dots, u_{nn}), \U = \D\L	p \qquad	ext{and}\qquad \A = \L\D\L	p . Pull the diagonal out of \U, writing \U = \D\V with \V unit upper triangular, so \A = \L\D\V. Transposing, \A = \A	p = \V	p (\D\L	p), which is a second LU factorization of the same invertible matrix. Uniqueness forces the two to ag