Positive Matrices in Blocks Chapter 8 cut a matrix into blocks, ran one step of elimination, and gave a name to what was left in the far corner: the Schur complement. Section 1 of this chapter met that same quantity as a single number, in the induction that proved Sylvester’s criterion. This section puts the two together. For a Hermitian matrix split into blocks, positive definiteness can be decided one block at a time, and the deciding quantity is the Schur complement. The test is then spent on a question Chapter 12 left open: which matrices carry a positive definite solution of the Lyapunov equation, a certificate of stability that names no eigenvalue and computes none. What the same block decomposition says about determinants is the next section’s subject. Throughout, F = 
