e \cvec{0}. The map \varphi is linear, being given by deleting a coordinate, and it is bijective from H to F^{n-1} because inserting 0 in position m inverts it. Write I = \{i_1 < \dots < i_{n-1}\}, so that \varphi(\cvec{x})_r = x_{i_r} and (\B)_{rs} = a_{i_r i_s} by @def-submatrix-minor. Let \cvec{x} \in H. Expanding the inner product of F^n, which is linear in the first slot and conjugate-linear in the second, \inner{\A\cvec{x}}{\cvec{x}} = \sum_{p=1}^{n}\sum_{q=1}^{n} a_{pq}\,x_q\,\conj{x_p} . Every term with p = m or q = m vanishes, because x_m = 0. The remaining terms are indexed by p, q \in I, say p = i_r and q = i_s, and there the summand is a_{i_ri_s}\varphi(\cvec{x})_s\conj{\varphi(\cvec{x})_r} = (\B)_{rs}\varphi(\cvec{x})_s\conj{\varphi(\cvec{x})_r}. Summing over r, s gives \inner{\B\varphi(\cvec{x})}{\varphi(\cvec{x})}. The same deletion of a zero coordinate gives 
