Krylov Subspaces Sections 4 and 5 both consist of multiplying by \A over and over: the power method forms \cvec{b}, \A\cvec{b}, \A^2\cvec{b}, \dots and keeps only the newest vector, and the QR algorithm, by @thm-qr-is-orthogonal-iteration, does the same thing to \cvec{e}_1 inside its first column. In both cases everything computed before the last step is thrown away, and this section keeps it instead. The span of the vectors produced by k matrix-vector products is a subspace of dimension up to k, and the best approximations it contains are far better than its last vector alone. That subspace, the process that builds an orthonormal basis of it, and the eigenvalue estimates it yields are the subject here. Throughout, F = 
R or F = 
C, and F^n carries the standard inner product, linear in the first slot, with \inner{\cvec{x}}{\cvec{y}} = \cvec{y}^{*}\cvec{x} and 
orm{\cdot} = 
orm{\cdot}_2. The space that k products span A large matrix is often not available as a table of n^2 numbers at all. What is available is a rule for computing \A\cvec{x} from \cvec{x} — a sparse pattern of neighbors on a grid, a graph, a differential operator discretized. For such a matrix, forming \A\cvec{x} is cheap and forming \A^{-1}, or even looking at an entry of \A^2, is not. So the honest question is: after k multiplications, what do we possess? We possess \cvec{b}, \A\cvec{b}, \dots, \A^{k-1}\cvec{b}, and hence every linear combination of them. The power method uses one of these vectors. That is wasteful, and the waste has a name only because the span deserves one. The Krylov space is everything that k matrix-vector products can reach. [Krylov Subspace] Let \A \in M_n(F), let \cvec{b} \in F^n, and let k \ge 1 be an integer. The k-th Krylov subspace of the pair (\A, \cvec{b}) is \cK_k(\A, \cvec{b}) \coloneqq \Span igl(\cvec{b},\ \A\cvec{b},\ \A^2\cvec{b},\ \dots,\ \A^{k-1}\cvec{b} igr) \subseteq F^n . In words: we list the first k vectors of the sequence obtained by repeatedly applying \A to \cvec{b} — starting at \cvec{b} itself, so that the last one is \A^{k-1}\cvec{b} and not \A^{k}\cvec{b} — and take their span. It is a subspace for the usual reason, being a span. The index k counts vectors, not powers; the off-by-one is a convention, and it is the one that makes \dim\cK_k \le k with equality in the typical case. Examples. Take F = 
R throughout. - The identity. For \A = \I_n and any \cvec{b} 
eq \cvec{0}, every \A^{j}\cvec{b} equals \cvec{b}, so \cK_k(\I_n, \cvec{b}) = \Span(\cvec{b}) for every k. One product buys nothing, and neither do a hundred. - A companion matrix. Let \A\cvec{e}_j = \cvec{e}_{j+1} for j \le n-1, as for the companion matrix of @def-companion-matrix, and let \cvec{b} = \cvec{e}_1. Then \A^{j}\cvec{e}_1 = \cvec{e}_{j+1} for j \le n-1, so \cK_k(\A, \cvec{e}_1) = \Span(\cvec{e}_1, \dots, \cvec{e}_k) for k \le n, of dimension exactly k. Here every product buys a new dimension. - A diagonal matrix. Let \A = \diag(4, 3, 2, 1) and \cvec{b} = (1,1,1,1). Then \A^{j}\cvec{b} = (4^{j}, 3^{j}, 2^{j}, 1), and these four vectors for j = 0, 1, 2, 3 form a Vandermonde matrix with distinct nodes, hence are independent (@thm-vandermonde-determinant). So \dim\cK_k = k for k \le 4, and \cK_4 = 
R^4. - Degenerate cases. \cK_1(\A, \cvec{b}) = \Span(\cvec{b}); and \cK_k(\A, \cvec{0}) = \{\cvec{0}\} for every k, which is why \cvec{b} 
eq \cvec{0} is assumed from here on. Nonexample by minimal change. Drop the middle vector of the list. Let \A \in M_3(
R) satisfy \A\cvec{e}_1 = \cvec{e}_2, \A\cvec{e}_2 = \cvec{e}_3, \A\cvec{e}_3 = \cvec{0}, and let \cvec{b} = \cvec{e}_1. The set \Span(\cvec{b}, \A^2\cvec{b}) = \Span(\cvec{e}_1, \cvec{e}_3) is a perfectly good subspace, and it is not \cK_k(\A,\cvec{b}) for any k, since those are \Span(\cvec{e}_1), \Span(\cvec{e}_1,\cvec{e}_2), and 
R^3. The clause that fails is that the powers be consecutive, and what it costs is everything: \A\Span(\cvec{e}_1,\cvec{e}_3) = \Span(\cvec{e}_2), which meets \Span(\cvec{e}_1,\cvec{e}_3) only at \cvec{0}. The Krylov spaces, by contrast, are nested and almost invariant, which is the next proposition. Why this definition. Chapter 10 already met the union of all of them. The cyclic subspace Z(\cvec{b}; \A) = \Span(\cvec{b}, \A\cvec{b}, \A^2\cvec{b}, \dots) of @def-cyclic-subspace is the smallest \A-invariant subspace containing \cvec{b} (@prp-cyclic-subspace-smallest), and @thm-cyclic-subspace-basis says that the list stops growing exactly at the degree of the \A-annihilator m_{\A,\cvec{b}}. The Krylov space is that construction truncated at k, and the truncation is the whole point: Z(\cvec{b};\A) is a theoretical object, while \cK_k(\A,\cvec{b}) is what k units of work actually deliver. [Krylov Spaces: Nesting, Dimension, Invariance] Let \A \in M_n(F) and \cvec{b} \in F^n with \cvec{b} 
eq \cvec{0}, and let d = \deg m_{\A,\cvec{b}} \ge 1 be the degree of the \A-annihilator of \cvec{b} (@def-t-annihilator). Then, for every k \ge 1: 1. \cK_k(\A,\cvec{b}) = \{\, p(\A)\cvec{b} : p \in F[x],\ \deg p \le k-1 \,\}, and \cK_k \subseteq \cK_{k+1} and \A\,\cK_k \subseteq \cK_{k+1}; 2. \dim\cK_k(\A,\cvec{b}) = \min(k, d); 3. \cK_{k+1} = \cK_k if and only if k \ge d, and in that case \cK_k = Z(\cvec{b};\A) is \A-invariant. Everything is @thm-cyclic-subspace-basis read one index at a time. That theorem says (\cvec{b}, \A\cvec{b}, \dots, \A^{d-1}\cvec{b}) is a basis of Z(\cvec{b};\A); so the first d vectors of our list are independent, and from \A^{d}\cvec{b} on they add nothing. (a) A linear combination \sum_{j<k} a_j\A^{j}\cvec{b} is p(\A)\cvec{b} for p = \sum_{j<k}a_jx^{j}, and conversely, which is the first statement. The inclusion \cK_k \subseteq \cK_{k+1} holds because the spanning list of \cK_k is part of that of \cK_{k+1}. And \A\A^{j}\cvec{b} = \A^{j+1}\cvec{b} \in \cK_{k+1} for j \le k-1, so \A maps a spanning list of \cK_k into \cK_{k+1}, hence maps \cK_k into \cK_{k+1}. (b) By @thm-cyclic-subspace-basis (a), the list (\cvec{b}, \A\cvec{b}, \dots, \A^{d-1}\cvec{b}) is a basis of Z(\cvec{b};\A). If k \le d, the first k entries of that list are a sublist of a basis, hence independent, so \dim\cK_k = k. If k > d, then \cK_k \subseteq Z(\cvec{b};\A), because every \A^{j}\cvec{b} lies there (@prp-cyclic-subspace-smallest (a)); and \cK_k \supseteq \cK_d = Z(\cvec{b};\A), since \cK_d already contains a basis of it. So \cK_k = Z(\cvec{b};\A) and \dim\cK_k = d. In both cases \dim\cK_k = \min(k,d). (c) (\Leftarrow) If k \ge d, then (b) gives \dim\cK_{k+1} = d = \dim\cK_k, and with \cK_k \subseteq \cK_{k+1} from (a) this forces equality; and the common space is Z(\cvec{b};\A), \A-invariant by @prp-cyclic-subspace-smallest (b). (\Rightarrow) If k < d, then (b) gives \dim\cK_{k+1} = k+1 > k = \dim\cK_k, so the two differ. This proves the proposition. The list \cvec{b}, \A\cvec{b}, \dots, \A^{k-1}\cvec{b} is a basis of \cK_k and is useless as one. By Section 4’s reading of the power method (@thm-power-method), \A^{j}\cvec{b} turns towards the dominant eigenvector as j grows, so the later vectors of the list are nearly parallel to each other. Even for \A = \diag(4,3,2,1) and \cvec{b} = (1,1,1,1), with its modest ratio \lambda_1/\lambda_2 = 4/3, the angle between consecutive vectors of the list falls from 24 degrees to 8.9 degrees across those four vectors, and the matrix with those four columns has 2-norm condition number about 1200 — for a 4 	imes 4 problem with one-digit data. Independent they may be; in the arithmetic of @def-floating-point-model the coordinates of a vector in that basis are computed with errors magnified by that condition number, which grows quickly with k. A Krylov method never uses this basis. It builds an orthonormal one instead, and that is the next subsection. Let \A \in M_n(F) be invertible with \A^2 = \I_n, and let \cvec{b} 
